  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 1230 AM MDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nMuch more active weather is expected to move into the region \nstarting on Friday, and lasting into early next week.\n\nOn Friday, a deep upper low will slowly move down along the west \ncoast, while a secondary low moves towards California, getting \nembedded in a broad upper trough. Flow aloft over our region will \npick up out of the southwest, bringing good moisture transport into \nthe mountains. Showers will be isolated to scattered in nature to \nbegin with, mainly falling over the San Juans. Meanwhile, a \nshortwave will pass over the southern portion of our CWA, which \ncould bring some scattered afternoon showers to the far eastern \nplains. That will most likely depend on the speed of the wave as it \npushes past, with most of the heavier accumulations falling in \nKansas and the OK Panhandle.\n\nMoving into the weekend, the forecast gets less certain as model \nsolution begin to diverge. The general trends have the northern low \npushing southeast towards Nevada into Sunday, while the southern low \nmoves onshore in California. Beyond this point, the two low \npressures will phase with the broad flow pattern, namely the \ndeepening trough over the western CONUS, before pushing east and \ninto our area. That being said, the exact timing, path, and \namplitude of the systems as they interact are still too wildly \ndifferent between solutions for any fine details to be decided on \njust yet.\n\nIn spite of the wide arrange of synoptic patterns we could see into \nearly next week, the trend of impacts has started to take shape. QPF \nspread amongst latest guidance shows the highest accumulations in \nthe San Juans, no surprise there given the persistent SW flow, \nfollowed by the central mountains, with the rest of the higher \nterrain varying a bit more. Snow levels are still uncertain, given \nthat even a subtle shift north or south of the approaching low could \nimpact how much cold air moves into the region. In general, we can \nexpect heavier snow over the San Juans, mainly closer to the peaks. \nUncertainty increases even more looking further east, as the path of \nthe system will greatly influence whether the Palmer Divide and \neastern plains receive any precipitation at all, with deterministic \nguidance varying considerably.\n\nRegardless, latest models show plenty of moisture moving into the \nmountains through Sunday, with activity spreading east onto the \nplains through Tuesday. Temperatures will also cool off another 10-\n15 degrees or so by then thanks to an influx of cooler air. Beyond \nthat, models still need more time to agree on the details.",
